      ],
      "source": "https://georgetownohio.gov/"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Georgetown’s Grant Boyhood Home and schoolhouse are preserved landmarks that let visitors glimpse early American life.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://georgetownohio.gov/"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The town’s historic theater opened in 1907 as a vaudeville house, was first to show “talkies” and stayed in use until 1962.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        8
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.achp.gov/preserve-america/community/georgetown-ohio"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The New Hope Road Bridge, built in the 1870s, remains Ohio’s longest single covered bridge.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        9
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.achp.gov/preserve-america/community/georgetown-ohio"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Georgetown is the childhood home of U.S. Civil War general Ulysses S. Grant.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
